summary:
Česnavičius and Scholze prove purity for flat cohomology: for a Noetherian local complete intersection (R, 𝔪) and a commutative finite flat R-group G, H^i_𝔪(R, G) = 0 for i < dim(R), and for i ≤ dim(R) when R is regular and not a field. Consequences settle Gabber's conjectures (Pic(U_R)_tors = 0 in dimension ≥ 3; Br(R) ≅ Br(U_R) in dimension ≥ 4 or regular of dimension ≥ 2) and give purity for the Brauer group of schemes with complete intersection singularities.

The proof:
- reduces prime-to-p coefficients to Gabber–Thomason purity, reproved by algebraic tilting through arc descent, and a local Lefschetz theorem, which gives the étale depth bound vdim(R);
- relates flat cohomology of a perfectoid ring to coherent cohomology of A_inf through prismatic Dieudonné modules (the key formula), proved by p-complete arc descent for both sides;
- builds, over animated rings, excision, p-adic and general continuity, fpqc descent and Henselian invariance for fppf cohomology with finite locally free coefficients, from the crystalline case in characteristic p;
- uses an ind-syntomic André lemma to adjoin p-power roots of the equations and perfectoid purity to conclude.

The atlas plans integral perfectoid rings and André's lemma (PerfectoidQuotients Q0, Q3), perfect prisms (PR.0), animation and the cotangent complex (EnhancedDerivedSheaves E5, DerivedDeRhamCohomology DD.0–DD.1), the fppf site (SF.2), depth and complete intersections (R03.3) and smooth purity (EDC.2). It has no flat cohomology theory, Brauer groups of schemes, absolute purity or purity theorems.

Routes: a new roadmap for the purity theory; Part IIs of SchemeAndStackFoundations (flat cohomology over animated rings), PerfectoidQuotients (structure of integral perfectoid rings) and EtaleDualityAndPerverseSheaves (absolute purity); consumer routes to the proposed ArcTopologyAndDescent and prismatic Dieudonné Part II; and the Cohen structure theorem as a source of R03.1. Fourteen mistakes are recorded: thirteen misprints and a false edge case (d = 1) of Theorem 7.1.2.
